WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:02.656 --> 00:07.656 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

00:08.926 --> 00:12.686 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:13.556 --> 00:16.336 align:middle
Today the Federal Open
Market Committee decided

00:16.336 --> 00:19.586 align:middle
to raise the target range
for the federal funds rate

00:20.026 --> 00:22.116 align:middle
by 1/4 percentage point,

00:22.616 --> 00:25.736 align:middle
bringing it to 1/2
to 3/4 percent.

00:26.666 --> 00:28.646 align:middle
In doing so, my colleagues

00:28.646 --> 00:32.386 align:middle
and I are recognizing the
considerable progress the

00:32.386 --> 00:35.446 align:middle
economy has made toward
our dual objectives

00:35.526 --> 00:38.006 align:middle
of maximum employment
and price stability.

00:38.816 --> 00:40.146 align:middle
Over the past year,

00:40.376 --> 00:44.456 align:middle
2-1/4 million net new
jobs have been created,

00:44.966 --> 00:49.336 align:middle
unemployment has fallen further,
and inflation has moved closer

00:49.336 --> 00:52.706 align:middle
to our longer-run
goal of 2 percent.

00:53.696 --> 00:57.056 align:middle
We expect the economy will
continue to perform well,

00:57.586 --> 00:59.846 align:middle
with the job market
strengthening further

01:00.126 --> 01:02.386 align:middle
and inflation rising
to 2 percent

01:02.386 --> 01:04.166 align:middle
over the next couple of years.

01:04.936 --> 01:08.006 align:middle
I'll have more to say about
monetary policy shortly,

01:08.506 --> 01:11.526 align:middle
but first I'll review
recent economic developments

01:11.526 --> 01:12.326 align:middle
and the outlook.

01:13.126 --> 01:16.566 align:middle
Economic growth has picked up
since the middle of the year.

01:17.246 --> 01:20.896 align:middle
Household spending continues
to rise at a moderate pace,

01:21.196 --> 01:25.386 align:middle
supported by income gains
and by relatively high levels

01:25.386 --> 01:27.346 align:middle
of consumer sentiment
and wealth.

01:28.296 --> 01:29.806 align:middle
Business investment, however,

01:29.806 --> 01:33.106 align:middle
remains soft despite
some stabilization

01:33.106 --> 01:34.316 align:middle
in the energy sector.

01:35.286 --> 01:40.236 align:middle
Overall, we expect the economy
will expand at a moderate pace

01:40.236 --> 01:41.786 align:middle
over the next few years.

01:42.846 --> 01:46.476 align:middle
Job gains averaged
nearly 180,000 per month

01:46.476 --> 01:50.276 align:middle
over the past three months,
maintaining the solid pace

01:50.276 --> 01:52.556 align:middle
that we've seen since the
beginning of the year.

01:53.506 --> 01:56.386 align:middle
Over the past seven
years, since the depths

01:56.386 --> 01:57.686 align:middle
of the Great Recession,

01:58.216 --> 02:01.926 align:middle
more than 15 million jobs have
been added to the U.S. economy.

02:02.976 --> 02:06.586 align:middle
The unemployment rate fell
to 4.6 percent in November,

02:06.936 --> 02:11.026 align:middle
the lowest level since 2007,
prior to the recession.

02:12.086 --> 02:15.596 align:middle
Broader measures of labor market
slack have also moved lower,

02:16.206 --> 02:19.936 align:middle
and participation in the labor
force has been little changed,

02:19.936 --> 02:23.716 align:middle
on net, for about two
years now, a further sign

02:23.746 --> 02:25.106 align:middle
of improved conditions

02:25.106 --> 02:28.746 align:middle
in the labor market given
the underlying downward trend

02:28.746 --> 02:32.266 align:middle
in participation stemming
largely from the aging

02:32.266 --> 02:33.726 align:middle
of the U.S. population.

02:34.956 --> 02:36.516 align:middle
Looking ahead, we expect

02:36.516 --> 02:39.526 align:middle
that job conditions will
strengthen somewhat further.

02:40.416 --> 02:43.136 align:middle
Turning to inflation,
the 12-month change

02:43.136 --> 02:47.156 align:middle
in the price index for personal
consumption expenditures was

02:47.156 --> 02:49.676 align:middle
nearly 1-1/2 percent in October,

02:50.156 --> 02:54.096 align:middle
still short of our 2
percent objective but up more

02:54.096 --> 02:56.656 align:middle
than a percentage point
from a year earlier.

02:57.616 --> 03:02.826 align:middle
Core inflation-which excludes
energy and food prices that tend

03:02.826 --> 03:06.076 align:middle
to be more volatile than
other prices-has risen

03:06.076 --> 03:07.866 align:middle
to 1-3/4 percent.

03:08.656 --> 03:12.286 align:middle
As the transitory
influences of earlier declines

03:12.286 --> 03:16.896 align:middle
in energy prices and prices
of imports continue to fade

03:17.376 --> 03:19.986 align:middle
and as the job market
strengthens further,

03:20.326 --> 03:24.196 align:middle
we expect overall inflation
to rise to 2 percent

03:24.196 --> 03:25.756 align:middle
over the next couple of years.

03:27.186 --> 03:30.796 align:middle
Our inflation outlook rests
importantly on our judgment

03:30.826 --> 03:35.466 align:middle
that longer-run inflation
expectations remain reasonably

03:35.466 --> 03:36.276 align:middle
well anchored.

03:37.196 --> 03:38.566 align:middle
Market-based measures

03:38.566 --> 03:40.856 align:middle
of inflation compensation
have moved

03:40.856 --> 03:43.396 align:middle
up considerably but
are still low.

03:44.046 --> 03:45.586 align:middle
Survey-based measures

03:45.586 --> 03:48.296 align:middle
of longer-run inflation
expectations are,

03:48.296 --> 03:50.516 align:middle
on balance, little changed.

03:51.196 --> 03:52.926 align:middle
Of course, we remain committed

03:52.926 --> 03:56.756 align:middle
to our 2 percent inflation
objective and will continue

03:56.756 --> 03:59.016 align:middle
to carefully monitor actual

03:59.016 --> 04:01.416 align:middle
and expected progress
toward this goal.

04:02.596 --> 04:06.376 align:middle
Let me now turn to the economic
projections that were submitted

04:06.376 --> 04:08.886 align:middle
for this meeting by
Committee participants.

04:09.956 --> 04:12.896 align:middle
As always, they conditioned
their projections

04:12.896 --> 04:16.706 align:middle
on their own individual views
of appropriate monetary policy,

04:17.356 --> 04:21.206 align:middle
which, in turn, depend on
each participant's assessment

04:21.426 --> 04:24.136 align:middle
of the multitude of factors
that shape the outlook.

04:25.116 --> 04:27.066 align:middle
The median projection for growth

04:27.066 --> 04:31.376 align:middle
of inflation-adjusted gross
domestic product rises

04:31.376 --> 04:36.966 align:middle
from 1.9 percent this year
to 2.1 percent in 2017

04:37.366 --> 04:41.236 align:middle
and stays close to 2
percent in 2018 and 2019,

04:41.676 --> 04:44.656 align:middle
slightly above its
estimated longer-run rate.

04:45.666 --> 04:48.366 align:middle
The median projection for
the unemployment rate stands

04:48.366 --> 04:52.566 align:middle
at 4.7 percent in the
fourth quarter of this year.

04:53.416 --> 04:57.736 align:middle
Over the next three years, the
median unemployment rate runs

04:57.896 --> 05:02.546 align:middle
at 4.5 percent, modestly
below the median estimate

05:02.546 --> 05:04.456 align:middle
of its longer-run normal rate.

05:05.186 --> 05:10.316 align:middle
Finally, the median inflation
projection is 1.5 percent this

05:10.316 --> 05:13.646 align:middle
year and rises to
1.9 percent next year

05:13.916 --> 05:16.906 align:middle
and 2 percent in 2018 and 2019.

05:17.866 --> 05:22.056 align:middle
Overall, these economic
projections are very similar

05:22.096 --> 05:27.176 align:middle
to those made in September:
GDP growth is a touch stronger;

05:27.676 --> 05:31.586 align:middle
the unemployment rate is a
shade lower; and inflation,

05:31.586 --> 05:33.426 align:middle
beyond this year, is unchanged.

05:34.396 --> 05:37.866 align:middle
Returning to monetary
policy, the Committee judged

05:37.866 --> 05:39.126 align:middle
that a modest increase

05:39.126 --> 05:42.446 align:middle
in the federal funds rate
is appropriate in light

05:42.446 --> 05:45.346 align:middle
of the solid progress we
have seen toward our goals

05:45.636 --> 05:48.496 align:middle
of maximum employment
and 2 percent inflation.

05:48.696 --> 05:51.766 align:middle
We continue to expect
that the evolution

05:51.766 --> 05:55.586 align:middle
of the economy will warrant
only gradual increases

05:55.586 --> 05:59.036 align:middle
in the federal funds
rate over time to achieve

05:59.036 --> 06:00.936 align:middle
and maintain our objectives.

06:01.596 --> 06:02.926 align:middle
That's based on our view

06:02.926 --> 06:06.906 align:middle
that the neutral nominal
federal funds rate-that is,

06:06.906 --> 06:09.636 align:middle
the interest rate that is
neither expansionary nor

06:09.636 --> 06:13.286 align:middle
contractionary and keeps
the economy operating

06:13.286 --> 06:17.026 align:middle
on an even keel-is
currently quite low

06:17.026 --> 06:18.756 align:middle
by historical standards.

06:19.576 --> 06:23.186 align:middle
With the federal funds rate only
modestly below the neutral rate,

06:23.456 --> 06:26.626 align:middle
we continue to expect
that gradual increases

06:26.936 --> 06:30.706 align:middle
in the federal funds rate will
likely be sufficient to get

06:30.706 --> 06:34.176 align:middle
to a neutral policy stance
over the next few years.

06:35.026 --> 06:38.516 align:middle
This view is consistent with
participants' projections

06:38.696 --> 06:40.736 align:middle
of appropriate monetary policy.

06:41.696 --> 06:45.126 align:middle
The median projection for
the federal funds rate rises

06:45.126 --> 06:48.656 align:middle
to 1.4 percent at
the end of next year,

06:49.246 --> 06:52.486 align:middle
2.1 percent at the end of 2018,

06:52.986 --> 06:56.216 align:middle
and 2.9 percent by
the end of 2019.

06:57.166 --> 06:59.706 align:middle
Compared with the
projections made in September,

07:00.106 --> 07:03.946 align:middle
the median path for the federal
funds rate has been revised

07:03.946 --> 07:06.156 align:middle
up just ¼ percentage point.

07:07.026 --> 07:09.906 align:middle
Only a few participants
altered their estimate

07:09.906 --> 07:12.536 align:middle
of the longer-run normal
federal funds rate,

07:13.156 --> 07:15.706 align:middle
although the median
edged up to 3 percent.

07:16.876 --> 07:19.816 align:middle
Of course, the economic
outlook is highly uncertain,

07:20.286 --> 07:23.026 align:middle
and participants will
adjust their assessments

07:23.026 --> 07:27.516 align:middle
of the appropriate path for the
federal funds rate in response

07:27.816 --> 07:31.596 align:middle
to changes to the economic
outlook and associated risks.

07:32.236 --> 07:36.386 align:middle
As many observers have noted,
changes in fiscal policy

07:36.386 --> 07:40.186 align:middle
or other economic policies
could potentially affect the

07:40.186 --> 07:41.296 align:middle
economic outlook.

07:42.036 --> 07:43.806 align:middle
Of course, it is far too early

07:43.806 --> 07:46.276 align:middle
to know how these
policies will unfold.

07:47.076 --> 07:50.646 align:middle
Moreover, changes in
fiscal policy are only one

07:50.646 --> 07:53.606 align:middle
of the many factors that
can influence the outlook

07:53.926 --> 07:56.676 align:middle
and the appropriate
course of monetary policy.

07:57.686 --> 07:59.726 align:middle
In making our policy decisions,

07:59.726 --> 08:04.156 align:middle
we will continue-as always-to
assess economic conditions

08:04.436 --> 08:08.026 align:middle
relative to our objectives
of maximum employment

08:08.026 --> 08:09.636 align:middle
and 2 percent inflation.

08:10.376 --> 08:12.506 align:middle
As I have noted on
previous occasions,

08:12.746 --> 08:15.296 align:middle
policy is not on
a preset course.

08:16.066 --> 08:19.096 align:middle
Finally, we will continue
to reinvest proceeds

08:19.096 --> 08:21.936 align:middle
from maturing Treasury
securities

08:21.936 --> 08:24.506 align:middle
and principal payments
from agency debt

08:24.756 --> 08:26.586 align:middle
and mortgage-backed securities.

08:27.136 --> 08:28.446 align:middle
As our statement says,

08:28.676 --> 08:32.896 align:middle
we anticipate continuing this
policy "until normalization

08:32.896 --> 08:36.586 align:middle
of the level of the federal
funds rate is well under way."

08:37.386 --> 08:38.016 align:middle
Thank you.

08:38.366 --> 08:40.566 align:middle
I'd be happy to take
your questions.

08:40.996 --> 08:42.726 align:middle
HARRIET TORRY.

08:44.636 --> 08:45.976 align:middle
Thank you, Madam Chair.

08:45.976 --> 08:47.436 align:middle
Harriet Torry with the
Wall Street Journal.

08:47.896 --> 08:51.336 align:middle
Why does the Fed now see three
rate increases next year instead

08:51.336 --> 08:51.816 align:middle
of two?

08:51.816 --> 08:54.266 align:middle
Is it because the economy
is at risk of overheating,

08:54.266 --> 08:57.066 align:middle
or is the Fed behind the
curve, or is this a reaction

08:57.066 --> 08:58.396 align:middle
to Donald Trump's election?

08:58.656 --> 08:59.176 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

08:59.176 --> 09:00.736 align:middle
Well, I would like to emphasize

09:00.736 --> 09:05.396 align:middle
that this is a very modest
adjustment in the path

09:05.396 --> 09:12.746 align:middle
of the federal funds rate
and involves changes by only,

09:12.966 --> 09:18.036 align:middle
you know, some of, you know,
some of the participants.

09:19.406 --> 09:25.046 align:middle
So in thinking about the
paths and the revisions,

09:25.046 --> 09:27.396 align:middle
there are a number of
factors that were taken

09:27.396 --> 09:29.776 align:middle
into account by participants.

09:30.276 --> 09:34.296 align:middle
The unemployment rate is
perhaps a touch, as I said,

09:34.296 --> 09:36.486 align:middle
a touch lower than previously;

09:36.486 --> 09:40.886 align:middle
you've seen some modest
downward revisions

09:40.886 --> 09:43.036 align:middle
in that-in that projection.

09:43.036 --> 09:47.076 align:middle
For this year, there was
a slight upward revision

09:47.076 --> 09:50.546 align:middle
to inflation, and some
of the participants,

09:50.586 --> 09:55.916 align:middle
but not all of the participants,
did incorporate some assumption

09:55.916 --> 09:59.846 align:middle
of a change in fiscal policy
into their projections.

10:00.156 --> 10:05.936 align:middle
And that may have been a
factor that was one of several

10:05.936 --> 10:08.966 align:middle
that occasioned these shifts,
but I want to emphasize

10:08.966 --> 10:13.076 align:middle
that these-the shifts that you
see here are really very tiny.

10:14.636 --> 10:16.126 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

10:18.986 --> 10:20.276 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

10:20.816 --> 10:23.386 align:middle
In recent testimony,
you said your advice was

10:23.846 --> 10:25.126 align:middle
for fiscal authorities

10:25.126 --> 10:27.726 align:middle
to increase the productive
capacity of the economy.

10:28.356 --> 10:31.576 align:middle
Do individual and business tax
cuts increase the productive

10:31.576 --> 10:32.906 align:middle
capacity of the economy?

10:33.276 --> 10:38.786 align:middle
And how would the Fed's reaction
be different to fiscal policies

10:38.786 --> 10:40.416 align:middle
that increase the
productive capacity

10:40.416 --> 10:45.196 align:middle
of the economy and
those that don't?

10:46.696 --> 10:47.446 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

10:47.446 --> 10:50.376 align:middle
So the statement that I
made, that it would be useful

10:50.546 --> 10:54.246 align:middle
to increase the productive
capacity of the economy,

10:54.656 --> 10:55.996 align:middle
reflects my concern

10:55.996 --> 10:58.936 align:middle
that productivity
growth has been very low.

10:59.286 --> 11:01.396 align:middle
It's the ultimate determination

11:01.396 --> 11:04.586 align:middle
of the evolution of
living standards.

11:05.646 --> 11:08.756 align:middle
Policies that would improve
productivity growth would

11:08.756 --> 11:15.556 align:middle
include policy changes that
enhance education, training,

11:15.816 --> 11:21.596 align:middle
workforce development; policies
that spur either private

11:21.596 --> 11:26.626 align:middle
or public investment to
enhance the quality of capital

11:26.626 --> 11:32.136 align:middle
in the United States that
workers have to work with;

11:32.456 --> 11:36.886 align:middle
and policies that spur
innovation or competition

11:36.886 --> 11:39.616 align:middle
or the formation of new firms.

11:40.146 --> 11:44.516 align:middle
So tax policies can
have that effect.

11:45.276 --> 11:48.196 align:middle
It really depends
on the specifics.

11:48.486 --> 11:51.676 align:middle
I don't think there's anything
that I could say in general

11:51.986 --> 11:54.686 align:middle
about what tax policy would do,

11:55.066 --> 12:03.566 align:middle
but that-and I really can't
tell you what the Fed's response

12:03.566 --> 12:07.856 align:middle
would be to any policy changes
that are put into effect.

12:08.086 --> 12:13.086 align:middle
I wouldn't want to speculate
until I were more certain

12:13.086 --> 12:17.016 align:middle
of the details and how they
would affect the likely course

12:17.016 --> 12:17.856 align:middle
of the economy.

12:18.056 --> 12:18.636 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

12:18.636 --> 12:22.916 align:middle
Okay. A quick follow-up-I'm
sorry, but if there was a rush

12:22.966 --> 12:26.756 align:middle
of fiscal policy that did not
increase the productive capacity

12:26.756 --> 12:29.696 align:middle
of the economy, would that mean
the Federal Reserve would have

12:29.696 --> 12:31.936 align:middle
to move more quickly
with raising rates?

12:31.936 --> 12:32.516 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

12:32.636 --> 12:35.596 align:middle
I-you know, it's something I
really just can't generalize

12:35.596 --> 12:40.676 align:middle
about because, while it would be
desirable to have tax policies

12:40.676 --> 12:44.016 align:middle
that do increase the productive
capacity of the economy,

12:44.016 --> 12:48.966 align:middle
an increase in the pace of
productivity change is one

12:48.966 --> 12:52.866 align:middle
of the factors that does affect
the economy's neutral rate.

12:53.176 --> 12:56.746 align:middle
A boost to productivity
could spur investment.

12:57.336 --> 13:01.906 align:middle
As we have been saying,
we estimate that the value

13:02.136 --> 13:07.536 align:middle
of the neutral federal funds
rate is quite low-has-and one

13:07.536 --> 13:11.636 align:middle
of the reasons for that is
slow productivity growth.

13:11.636 --> 13:14.586 align:middle
And so it's very hard
to generalize about it

13:14.646 --> 13:17.276 align:middle
because it could affect
that neutral rate.

13:18.796 --> 13:21.216 align:middle
JIM PUZZANGHERA.

13:22.506 --> 13:24.236 align:middle
Hi. Jim Puzzanghera
with the L.A. Times.

13:24.596 --> 13:27.136 align:middle
For the average American, can
you explain what the impact

13:27.136 --> 13:29.916 align:middle
of this hike and three
additional hikes will be

13:29.916 --> 13:30.596 align:middle
next year?

13:30.646 --> 13:34.226 align:middle
And should they feel more
confident in the economy now

13:34.226 --> 13:37.196 align:middle
that you are raising rates
at a slightly faster pace?

13:37.496 --> 13:37.926 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

13:37.926 --> 13:40.526 align:middle
So let me say that our decision

13:40.526 --> 13:45.686 align:middle
to raise rates is-should
certainly be understood

13:46.566 --> 13:52.526 align:middle
as a reflection of
the confidence we have

13:52.776 --> 13:57.166 align:middle
in the progress the economy
has made and our judgment

13:57.166 --> 13:59.306 align:middle
that that progress
will continue.

13:59.636 --> 14:04.256 align:middle
And the economy has proven
to be remarkably resilient.

14:04.256 --> 14:09.226 align:middle
So it is a vote of
confidence in the economy.

14:09.416 --> 14:14.316 align:middle
As you know, this was a decision
that was well anticipated

14:14.566 --> 14:19.866 align:middle
in markets, and I think it will
have relatively small effect

14:20.146 --> 14:22.006 align:middle
on market rates.

14:22.006 --> 14:27.406 align:middle
It could boost very slightly
some short-term interest rates

14:27.706 --> 14:31.926 align:middle
that could have an effect on
borrowing costs that are linked

14:31.926 --> 14:36.156 align:middle
to them, but, overall,
I think that households

14:36.156 --> 14:42.496 align:middle
and firms will see very modest
changes from this decision.

14:42.786 --> 14:46.486 align:middle
But, certainly, it's important
for households and businesses

14:46.486 --> 14:51.816 align:middle
to understand that my colleagues
and I have judged the course

14:51.816 --> 14:55.156 align:middle
of the U.S. economy
to be strong,

14:55.156 --> 14:58.926 align:middle
that we're making progress
toward our inflation

14:58.926 --> 15:00.376 align:middle
and unemployment goals.

15:00.656 --> 15:04.686 align:middle
We have a strong labor market,
and we have a resilient economy.

15:05.466 --> 15:07.296 align:middle
SAM FLEMING.

15:09.056 --> 15:10.986 align:middle
Sam Fleming from
the Financial Times.

15:11.026 --> 15:12.906 align:middle
Clearly, there's been a
lot of discussion already

15:12.906 --> 15:15.576 align:middle
about fiscal-fiscal policy.

15:15.576 --> 15:17.296 align:middle
But even if you discount

15:17.296 --> 15:19.556 align:middle
or don't know what the
fiscal outlook is going

15:19.586 --> 15:24.036 align:middle
to be next year, unemployment
is already below the longer-run

15:24.106 --> 15:28.436 align:middle
projection under the policy rule
you cited in your August speech.

15:28.436 --> 15:32.346 align:middle
That would suggest that
policy ought to be tighter.

15:33.426 --> 15:36.196 align:middle
Is there a risk that the Federal
Reserve is already behind the

15:36.196 --> 15:39.506 align:middle
curve, even before any fiscal
impact steps in next year?

15:39.816 --> 15:41.286 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR YELLEN.

15:41.286 --> 15:44.046 align:middle
So I would agree
that-and as we said

15:44.046 --> 15:47.176 align:middle
in our statement-policy
remains accommodative.

15:47.806 --> 15:53.156 align:middle
The degree of accommodation I
would characterize as moderate.

15:53.156 --> 15:57.416 align:middle
As I've emphasized and
said in the statement,

15:58.016 --> 16:00.316 align:middle
we currently judge
the neutral level

16:00.316 --> 16:03.366 align:middle
of the federal funds
rate to be pretty low.

16:03.746 --> 16:06.216 align:middle
So there is some accommodation.

16:06.516 --> 16:11.926 align:middle
Remember that inflation is
still below our objective.

16:12.436 --> 16:18.236 align:middle
The Committee projects-at least
the median projection shows a

16:18.236 --> 16:21.586 align:middle
very modest undershoot
of estimates

16:21.586 --> 16:24.826 align:middle
of the longer-run normal
rate of unemployment.

16:25.116 --> 16:29.956 align:middle
The median unemployment rate
here gets down to 4-1/2 percent,

16:30.226 --> 16:35.876 align:middle
which is just a few tenths below
the estimated longer-run normal

16:35.876 --> 16:37.766 align:middle
level of the unemployment rate.

16:38.166 --> 16:41.186 align:middle
And we think that
that's appropriate

16:41.246 --> 16:43.446 align:middle
because we want inflation
to rise

16:43.446 --> 16:47.436 align:middle
to our 2 percent objective
in a timely fashion.

16:47.706 --> 16:51.846 align:middle
So there is not a
substantial undershoot

16:51.846 --> 16:54.326 align:middle
of the natural rate
of unemployment.

16:54.816 --> 16:59.096 align:middle
We're not seeing
evidence in labor markets

16:59.386 --> 17:03.236 align:middle
of very substantial
upward pressures on labor

17:03.236 --> 17:08.616 align:middle
that could signify
extreme shortages of labor

17:08.616 --> 17:14.666 align:middle
that could propel inflation
higher in a very rapid way,

17:14.666 --> 17:18.136 align:middle
and inflation is still
operating below our objective.

17:18.496 --> 17:22.196 align:middle
So I do not judge that
we are behind the curve.

17:22.546 --> 17:26.216 align:middle
I've-my judgment is that
we're on a good path

17:26.396 --> 17:28.976 align:middle
to reaching our objectives.

17:29.216 --> 17:31.486 align:middle
But, of course, the
outlook is uncertain.

17:31.766 --> 17:34.286 align:middle
We recognize that
there are many sources

17:34.566 --> 17:37.156 align:middle
of uncertainty affecting
the outlook.

17:37.456 --> 17:43.316 align:middle
And we will have to adjust
our thinking as things evolve,

17:43.316 --> 17:46.736 align:middle
as conditions-conditions
evolve, and we learn more

17:47.036 --> 17:50.826 align:middle
about economic policy changes
that could affect the outlook.

17:51.436 --> 17:53.676 align:middle
JIM TANKERSLEY.

17:56.056 --> 17:58.816 align:middle
Jim Tankersley, Washington Post.

17:59.246 --> 18:01.696 align:middle
I'm curious, you and
your predecessor had both

18:01.786 --> 18:05.636 align:middle
at times called for more
fiscal stimulus to help

18:05.856 --> 18:07.476 align:middle
with the outlook,
the growth outlook.

18:07.476 --> 18:10.796 align:middle
And I'm wondering, how much
do you judge the economy has

18:10.796 --> 18:13.366 align:middle
capacity for fiscal
stimulus right now?

18:13.366 --> 18:15.086 align:middle
It's a version of
Steve's question,

18:15.086 --> 18:16.296 align:middle
but I think we're
trying to get at,

18:16.596 --> 18:19.856 align:middle
how much can happen before we
run that risk of overheating?

18:20.526 --> 18:21.276 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

18:21.306 --> 18:26.476 align:middle
Well, I believe my predecessor
and I called for fiscal stimulus

18:26.476 --> 18:30.086 align:middle
when the unemployment rate
was substantially higher

18:30.176 --> 18:31.276 align:middle
than it is now.

18:31.276 --> 18:35.356 align:middle
So, with a 4.6 percent
unemployment

18:35.356 --> 18:40.336 align:middle
and a solid labor market, there
may be some additional slack

18:40.616 --> 18:45.436 align:middle
in labor markets, but I
would judge that the degree

18:45.436 --> 18:47.526 align:middle
of slack has diminished.

18:47.936 --> 18:51.236 align:middle
So I would say at this point

18:51.316 --> 18:55.816 align:middle
that fiscal policy is
not obviously needed

18:55.816 --> 18:59.256 align:middle
to provide stimulus to help us
get back to full employment.

18:59.726 --> 19:03.906 align:middle
But, nevertheless, let me be
careful that I am not trying

19:03.906 --> 19:08.226 align:middle
to provide advice to the new
Administration or to Congress

19:08.226 --> 19:11.766 align:middle
as to what is the
appropriate stance of policy.

19:12.086 --> 19:15.726 align:middle
There are many considerations
that Congress needs

19:15.756 --> 19:20.266 align:middle
to take account of
and many bases

19:20.266 --> 19:24.636 align:middle
for justifying changing
fiscal policy.

19:24.996 --> 19:28.346 align:middle
I've continued to
highlight the importance

19:28.346 --> 19:31.896 align:middle
of spurring productivity
growth, that I think

19:31.896 --> 19:35.456 align:middle
that would be something that's
beneficial for the economy.

19:35.816 --> 19:39.826 align:middle
Of course, it's also important
for Congress to take account

19:39.826 --> 19:44.106 align:middle
of the fact that, as
our population ages,

19:44.406 --> 19:49.226 align:middle
that the debt-to-GDP ratio
is projected to rise,

19:49.226 --> 19:53.366 align:middle
and that needs to continue
to be taken into account.

19:53.456 --> 19:54.946 align:middle
And so there are many factors

19:54.946 --> 19:57.716 align:middle
that I think should enter
into such decisions.

19:58.376 --> 19:59.666 align:middle
CHRISTOPHER CONDON.

20:01.776 --> 20:02.326 align:middle
Thank you.

20:02.426 --> 20:04.566 align:middle
Chris Condon, Bloomberg News.

20:05.186 --> 20:07.206 align:middle
Chair Yellen, you've
just spoken about some

20:07.206 --> 20:09.746 align:middle
of the risks-the
inflationary risks of running

20:09.746 --> 20:11.706 align:middle
in this expansionary
fiscal policy.

20:12.186 --> 20:16.116 align:middle
But in October you were
wondering whether it might be

20:16.276 --> 20:19.446 align:middle
possible to repair
some of the damage done

20:19.486 --> 20:22.466 align:middle
to the labor force
during the recession

20:22.466 --> 20:25.586 align:middle
by running what you termed
a "high-pressure economy."

20:25.966 --> 20:32.086 align:middle
So I'm wondering, why couldn't
fiscal policy serve the same end

20:32.226 --> 20:35.336 align:middle
in seeking to run a
high-pressure economy,

20:35.336 --> 20:38.146 align:middle
hoping to draw more
Americans off the sidelines

20:38.476 --> 20:40.126 align:middle
and into the workforce?

20:40.676 --> 20:44.326 align:middle
Is there something necessarily
riskier about approaching it

20:44.326 --> 20:49.106 align:middle
from the fiscal side, or
perhaps have you become less

20:49.106 --> 20:52.416 align:middle
enthusiastic about the idea of
running a high-pressure economy?

20:52.496 --> 20:52.796 align:middle
Thank you.

20:52.796 --> 20:53.036 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

20:53.296 --> 20:56.266 align:middle
So I want to be clear that
what I said in that speech

20:56.266 --> 21:03.496 align:middle
in Boston is that an important
research question is whether

21:03.496 --> 21:07.946 align:middle
or not, in an economy with
a very strong labor market,

21:08.686 --> 21:12.236 align:middle
there might be changes
that took place

21:12.236 --> 21:19.326 align:middle
that permanently raised the
labor force participation,

21:19.326 --> 21:23.556 align:middle
training, and other things of
the labor-of the labor force

21:23.686 --> 21:27.686 align:middle
that would be positives for
the productive potential

21:27.686 --> 21:30.666 align:middle
of our economy on a
long-lasting basis.

21:30.886 --> 21:34.836 align:middle
I never said that I favor
running a high-pressure economy.

21:35.196 --> 21:41.816 align:middle
And, you know, as you can
see in the SEP projections

21:42.086 --> 21:46.496 align:middle
of the participants-and this
has long been true not just

21:46.766 --> 21:49.286 align:middle
in this forecast,
but in earlier ones

21:49.546 --> 21:53.786 align:middle
as well-you see a
modest undershooting.

21:53.786 --> 21:56.906 align:middle
The under-unemployment
rate is projected

21:57.166 --> 22:00.676 align:middle
to modestly undershoot,
for several years,

22:00.956 --> 22:05.966 align:middle
levels that are deemed to
be normal in the longer run.

22:06.336 --> 22:10.736 align:middle
That's an appropriate
policy purely on the grounds

22:10.736 --> 22:14.906 align:middle
that inflation is running
below our objective.

22:15.186 --> 22:16.746 align:middle
And while we don't want

22:16.746 --> 22:19.126 align:middle
to overshoot our 2
percent objective,

22:19.396 --> 22:22.116 align:middle
we also don't want a
persistent undershoot

22:22.526 --> 22:28.906 align:middle
of our 2 percent objective, and
that does involve a labor market

22:28.996 --> 22:34.476 align:middle
that may succeed in attracting
more people off the sidelines

22:34.476 --> 22:35.636 align:middle
into the labor market.

22:35.636 --> 22:42.006 align:middle
It's something we will see
as we examine experience

22:42.006 --> 22:43.656 align:middle
over the next couple of years.

22:43.696 --> 22:45.506 align:middle
We may adjust our views on this.

22:45.756 --> 22:50.816 align:middle
But I do want to make clear that
I have not recommended running a

22:50.816 --> 22:53.296 align:middle
"hot" economy as some
sort of experiment.

22:54.186 --> 22:55.246 align:middle
LINDSAY DUNSMUIR.

22:56.966 --> 22:58.316 align:middle
Hi, Lindsay Dunsmuir
with Reuters.

22:58.786 --> 23:02.206 align:middle
How comfortable are you
with possible interference

23:02.206 --> 23:04.716 align:middle
on Fed policy by the
incoming President?

23:04.786 --> 23:07.866 align:middle
And I'm talking there about
the negative impacts his tweets

23:07.866 --> 23:10.126 align:middle
on aerospace companies
in the last week have had

23:10.126 --> 23:11.006 align:middle
on their share prices.

23:11.526 --> 23:14.406 align:middle
And do you feel having
a President tweeting

23:14.406 --> 23:17.816 align:middle
about individual companies-do
you feel that that could begin

23:17.816 --> 23:19.416 align:middle
to distort corporate
decisionmaking?

23:19.836 --> 23:20.536 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

23:20.536 --> 23:25.246 align:middle
Well, I'm not going to offer
the incoming President advice

23:25.246 --> 23:29.836 align:middle
about how to conduct
himself in policy.

23:30.266 --> 23:33.576 align:middle
I'm a strong believer in
the independence of the Fed.

23:33.976 --> 23:38.656 align:middle
We have been given the
independence by Congress

23:38.766 --> 23:44.506 align:middle
to make decisions about
monetary policy in pursuit

23:44.506 --> 23:48.266 align:middle
of our dual-mandate
objectives of maximum employment

23:48.486 --> 23:53.716 align:middle
and inflation, and that is what
I intend to stay focused on.

23:53.716 --> 23:56.346 align:middle
That's what the Committee
is focused on.

23:57.616 --> 23:58.736 align:middle
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

23:59.336 --> 24:02.316 align:middle
Binya Appelbaum with
the New York Times.

24:02.316 --> 24:03.306 align:middle
The President-elect has said

24:03.306 --> 24:06.216 align:middle
that overhauling financial
regulation is a high priority

24:06.216 --> 24:06.706 align:middle
for him.

24:07.056 --> 24:11.336 align:middle
I'm curious whether the Fed has
been asked to provide any advice

24:11.336 --> 24:14.476 align:middle
on how that might be done, and
what advice you would provide

24:14.476 --> 24:17.486 align:middle
to the President-elect about how
our financial regulatory system

24:17.486 --> 24:18.176 align:middle
should be improved?

24:18.476 --> 24:19.846 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

24:20.166 --> 24:24.716 align:middle
So we have been-our
staff have been in touch

24:24.976 --> 24:27.696 align:middle
with the Trump transition team.

24:28.386 --> 24:31.376 align:middle
And we, of course,
share the objective

24:31.526 --> 24:34.986 align:middle
that the whole government
has to work constructively

24:35.076 --> 24:37.776 align:middle
to ensure a smooth transition.

24:38.186 --> 24:41.206 align:middle
I've not been in
touch beyond that,

24:41.206 --> 24:43.406 align:middle
and it's not something
that I would expect.

24:44.026 --> 24:49.556 align:middle
But-I'm sorry, so what
would I- BINYAMIN APPLEBAUM.

24:49.556 --> 24:53.576 align:middle
I was saying, what
advice would you give

24:53.796 --> 24:55.666 align:middle
about how the system
should be improved?

24:55.666 --> 24:56.266 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

24:56.366 --> 24:57.396 align:middle
About how- BINYAMIN APPLEBAUM.

24:57.396 --> 24:58.896 align:middle
-on financial regulation.

24:59.806 --> 25:02.426 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

25:02.646 --> 25:04.246 align:middle
-the financial rate.

25:04.246 --> 25:08.376 align:middle
Yes. So, okay, on financial
regulation, I feel that we lived

25:08.376 --> 25:10.816 align:middle
through a devastating
financial crisis

25:10.816 --> 25:13.026 align:middle
that took a huge
toll on our economy.

25:13.026 --> 25:16.146 align:middle
And most members of Congress
and the public came away

25:16.146 --> 25:19.296 align:middle
from that experience
feeling that it was important

25:19.396 --> 25:23.986 align:middle
to take a set of steps that
would result in a safer

25:23.986 --> 25:26.246 align:middle
and stronger financial system.

25:26.606 --> 25:29.176 align:middle
And I feel that we
have done that.

25:29.176 --> 25:33.766 align:middle
That has been our mission
since the financial crisis

25:33.766 --> 25:36.446 align:middle
for the last six or seven years.

25:37.166 --> 25:40.926 align:middle
That's what Dodd-Frank
was designed to do.

25:41.196 --> 25:46.016 align:middle
I think it's very important
that we have reduced the odds

25:46.016 --> 25:49.046 align:middle
that a systemically
important firm could fail

25:49.046 --> 25:53.556 align:middle
by requiring higher
capital, higher liquidity,

25:53.916 --> 25:58.586 align:middle
by performing stress tests
that provide us another way

25:58.846 --> 26:05.496 align:middle
of ensuring that the firms
we count on to supply credit

26:05.496 --> 26:09.766 align:middle
to households and businesses
would be able to go on doing

26:09.766 --> 26:14.186 align:middle
that even in the face of
a severely adverse shock.

26:14.666 --> 26:20.246 align:middle
The firms-the largest firms
have a great deal more capital

26:20.246 --> 26:22.286 align:middle
than they did before the crisis.

26:22.616 --> 26:24.606 align:middle
Those are important changes.

26:25.556 --> 26:31.156 align:middle
We have placed the toughest
regulations on those firms

26:31.156 --> 26:33.116 align:middle
that are systemically important.

26:33.586 --> 26:37.986 align:middle
I would advise that-and
we have been trying

26:37.986 --> 26:41.436 align:middle
to do this-that it's
important to look for ways

26:41.436 --> 26:45.256 align:middle
to relieve regulatory
burden on community banks

26:45.476 --> 26:49.416 align:middle
and smaller institutions,
to tailor regulation

26:49.646 --> 26:54.886 align:middle
so that it's appropriate for
the systemic risk profile

26:54.886 --> 26:57.036 align:middle
of the particular institutions.

26:57.426 --> 27:00.596 align:middle
I think there was
broad agreement also

27:00.876 --> 27:03.746 align:middle
that we should end
"too big to fail,"

27:03.976 --> 27:09.046 align:middle
and that means not only
reducing the odds of the failure

27:09.116 --> 27:12.076 align:middle
of a systemically
important institution,

27:12.426 --> 27:17.406 align:middle
but also making sure that,
should such a firm fail,

27:17.676 --> 27:20.376 align:middle
that it could be resolved
in an orderly way.

27:20.716 --> 27:24.446 align:middle
And the living wills
process has been about that,

27:24.446 --> 27:28.466 align:middle
and I think we've made
considerable progress

27:28.466 --> 27:31.136 align:middle
in making sure that the largest

27:31.136 --> 27:34.406 align:middle
and most systemic firms
conduct their businesses

27:34.606 --> 27:39.816 align:middle
in a day-to-day way with some
thought about-with important

27:39.886 --> 27:43.556 align:middle
thinking in place about whether

27:43.556 --> 27:46.496 align:middle
or not the way they are
conducting their business would

27:46.496 --> 27:48.316 align:middle
aid resolution in the event

27:48.316 --> 27:51.576 align:middle
that they encountered a
severe negative shock.

27:51.896 --> 27:54.796 align:middle
So this is progress,
I would say,

27:54.836 --> 27:57.856 align:middle
it's very important
not to roll back.

27:58.226 --> 28:00.776 align:middle
There may be some changes
that could be made,

28:00.776 --> 28:06.106 align:middle
and we've suggested a few,
like eliminating the burden

28:06.106 --> 28:10.776 align:middle
of compliance with the Volcker
rule, or incentive compensation,

28:11.966 --> 28:15.636 align:middle
regulations for smaller banks,

28:15.736 --> 28:21.226 align:middle
or modestly raising
the threshold for banks

28:21.226 --> 28:25.306 align:middle
that are subject to enhanced
prudential supervision.

28:25.836 --> 28:31.036 align:middle
But I would urge that it's
important to keep this in place.

28:34.306 --> 28:36.756 align:middle
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

28:36.836 --> 28:38.716 align:middle
Marty Crutsinger with
the Associated Press.

28:39.056 --> 28:40.976 align:middle
The election of Donald
Trump seemed

28:40.976 --> 28:44.256 align:middle
to have sparked a major
reaction in financial markets:

28:44.586 --> 28:47.516 align:middle
stock prices at record highs,
the dollar has strengthened,

28:47.806 --> 28:49.656 align:middle
long-term interest
rates are higher.

28:50.036 --> 28:54.186 align:middle
The-did any of that get
discussed in the meeting

28:54.186 --> 28:57.206 align:middle
and did it-do you feel that
it will have any effect,

28:57.206 --> 28:59.826 align:middle
either negative or
positive, on things?

28:59.826 --> 29:02.636 align:middle
And also, did you have
any broader discussion

29:02.636 --> 29:05.926 align:middle
about Trump's economic
plan and what it might do

29:05.926 --> 29:07.706 align:middle
and how the Fed might
have to react?

29:08.396 --> 29:09.366 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

29:09.526 --> 29:12.836 align:middle
So we did discuss these
topics in our meeting today.

29:13.406 --> 29:18.166 align:middle
I would simply summarize
by saying

29:18.446 --> 29:23.356 align:middle
that all the FOMC
participants recognize

29:23.626 --> 29:26.706 align:middle
that there is considerable
uncertainty

29:27.016 --> 29:30.006 align:middle
about how economic
policies may change

29:30.296 --> 29:32.836 align:middle
and what effect they
will have on the economy.

29:33.256 --> 29:37.556 align:middle
And in so far as that will
affect monetary policy,

29:37.556 --> 29:41.526 align:middle
of course we will have to
factor those policies along

29:41.526 --> 29:46.286 align:middle
with many other things,
including the global environment

29:46.286 --> 29:48.986 align:middle
and oil prices and
other matters.

29:49.246 --> 29:53.136 align:middle
We will have to factor that
into our outlook and figure

29:53.136 --> 29:55.806 align:middle
out what is an appropriate
response.

29:56.676 --> 30:00.896 align:middle
But we're operating under
a cloud of uncertainty

30:00.946 --> 30:03.066 align:middle
at the moment, and
we have time to wait

30:03.126 --> 30:07.996 align:middle
to see what changes
occur and to factor those

30:07.996 --> 30:12.056 align:middle
into our decisionmaking as
we gain greater clarity.

30:12.396 --> 30:14.246 align:middle
You mentioned the market moves.

30:14.246 --> 30:18.706 align:middle
So I see the market moves
as implicit forecasts

30:18.706 --> 30:22.436 align:middle
about what impact these
policies are likely

30:22.436 --> 30:24.256 align:middle
to have on the economy.

30:24.656 --> 30:29.466 align:middle
The changes-the financial market
changes that you described,

30:29.836 --> 30:33.466 align:middle
particularly the increase in
stock prices, the increase

30:33.466 --> 30:37.046 align:middle
in longer-term rates, and the
strengthening of the dollar,

30:37.046 --> 30:40.996 align:middle
suggest that many market
participants anticipate

30:41.296 --> 30:43.966 align:middle
expansionary fiscal policies

30:44.006 --> 30:47.006 align:middle
that would raise
interest rates somewhat

30:47.006 --> 30:49.686 align:middle
in the United States
relative to abroad

30:50.056 --> 30:52.676 align:middle
and would cause a
strengthening in the dollar.

30:52.676 --> 30:56.026 align:middle
But market participants
were uncertain too,

30:56.336 --> 31:00.766 align:middle
and I would expect changes
in our understanding

31:00.766 --> 31:06.476 align:middle
of what is going to happen
to also affect market prices

31:07.306 --> 31:09.846 align:middle
in financial markets
as we move forward.

31:10.386 --> 31:12.656 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

31:13.756 --> 31:16.096 align:middle
Hi, Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

31:16.586 --> 31:21.966 align:middle
Wondering about slack,
when do you think the slack

31:21.966 --> 31:24.236 align:middle
in the labor market
will have worked its way

31:24.236 --> 31:27.616 align:middle
through so we're no
longer talking about it

31:27.616 --> 31:30.456 align:middle
at press conferences and
it's not such a big issue?

31:30.516 --> 31:31.126 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

31:31.126 --> 31:32.876 align:middle
So this is not something

31:32.876 --> 31:35.656 align:middle
that it's possible
to judge precisely.

31:36.166 --> 31:41.006 align:middle
My colleagues write down
their best estimates

31:41.006 --> 31:44.226 align:middle
of a normal longer-run
unemployment rate.

31:44.646 --> 31:49.706 align:middle
The median stands at 4.8
percent, so we're close,

31:49.746 --> 31:52.986 align:middle
possibly-the unemployment
rate right now is ever

31:52.986 --> 31:55.526 align:middle
so slightly below, but
in the neighborhood.

31:56.546 --> 32:00.926 align:middle
If we look at larger,
broader measures of slack,

32:01.216 --> 32:03.106 align:middle
like the U-6 measure

32:03.106 --> 32:08.266 align:middle
that includes involuntary
part-time employment and those

32:08.266 --> 32:10.826 align:middle
who are marginally
attached to the labor force,

32:11.146 --> 32:15.186 align:middle
they're slightly higher
than pre-recession levels,

32:15.186 --> 32:17.136 align:middle
but they've come
down considerably.

32:17.616 --> 32:22.206 align:middle
We look at a broad array of
indicators of the labor market,

32:22.206 --> 32:26.406 align:middle
and if you look at job
openings or the hires rate

32:26.406 --> 32:34.306 align:middle
or the quits rate or difficulty
of hiring workers as reported

32:34.306 --> 32:36.636 align:middle
in business surveys, you know,

32:36.716 --> 32:39.956 align:middle
I would say the labor
market looks a lot

32:40.376 --> 32:43.606 align:middle
like the way it did
before the recession,

32:43.606 --> 32:47.836 align:middle
that it's-we're roughly
comparable to 2007 levels

32:47.836 --> 32:51.896 align:middle
when we thought the, you know,
there was a normal amount

32:51.896 --> 32:53.446 align:middle
of slack in the labor market.

32:53.486 --> 32:57.906 align:middle
The labor market was in the
vicinity of maximum employment.

32:58.346 --> 33:00.026 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

33:00.556 --> 33:06.546 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Television and Radio.

33:06.886 --> 33:09.156 align:middle
Given President-elect
Trump's criticism

33:09.156 --> 33:12.216 align:middle
of your low-rate policy during
the campaign, like it or not,

33:12.486 --> 33:17.446 align:middle
market participants are going
to focus a lot in 2017 on you.

33:17.446 --> 33:20.156 align:middle
You've suggested you will serve
out your term, but I'm curious

33:20.156 --> 33:23.556 align:middle
if that statement still holds,
if you would like another term

33:23.556 --> 33:25.956 align:middle
as Fed Chair, if you
would accept another term

33:26.326 --> 33:29.406 align:middle
as Fed Chair, and would
you stay on the Committee

33:29.446 --> 33:31.066 align:middle
if you were not Fed Chair?

33:31.426 --> 33:32.316 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

33:32.316 --> 33:36.646 align:middle
So I guess the way I think
about it is that I was confirmed

33:36.646 --> 33:38.956 align:middle
by the Senate to
a four-year term.

33:39.466 --> 33:43.696 align:middle
The term of the Fed Chair was
not meant to coincide with that

33:43.696 --> 33:48.036 align:middle
of the President, and there
were good reasons for that, too.

33:49.016 --> 33:52.356 align:middle
It's part of ensuring the
independence of the Fed.

33:52.726 --> 33:57.536 align:middle
And so I do intend to serve
out my four-year term.

33:57.866 --> 34:03.886 align:middle
I haven't made any
decision about the future.

34:03.886 --> 34:08.286 align:middle
I, you know, I recognize I might
or might not be reappointed.

34:08.806 --> 34:12.446 align:middle
It's a decision that I don't
have to make and don't,

34:12.556 --> 34:14.636 align:middle
you know, don't have
thoughts on at this time.

34:14.636 --> 34:17.766 align:middle
And, as you said, I recognize
too that I could stay

34:17.766 --> 34:21.136 align:middle
on as a Board member, and that's
a decision for another day.

34:21.686 --> 34:25.656 align:middle
PETER BARNES.

34:25.726 --> 34:26.786 align:middle
Peter Barnes, Fox Business.

34:26.786 --> 34:28.136 align:middle
Just to follow up
on Marty's question

34:28.136 --> 34:31.426 align:middle
about the financial markets,
the Dow is about to hit 20,000.

34:31.756 --> 34:35.026 align:middle
It's up substantially since
the election on, apparently,

34:35.026 --> 34:37.916 align:middle
investor optimism about
potential-the impact

34:38.236 --> 34:42.016 align:middle
of President-elect Trump's
policies on the economy

34:42.016 --> 34:44.186 align:middle
and an improving economy.

34:44.356 --> 34:47.306 align:middle
I wonder if you share that
optimism, number one, and,

34:47.306 --> 34:50.336 align:middle
if not, are we seeing
a bout of, perhaps,

34:50.336 --> 34:52.776 align:middle
irrational exuberance right now?

34:53.046 --> 34:57.906 align:middle
Or are you concerned at all
about a bubble in equity prices

34:57.906 --> 35:01.496 align:middle
that could create some financial
instability in the economy?

35:01.856 --> 35:02.116 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

35:02.346 --> 35:04.726 align:middle
So, you know, I really
don't want to comment

35:04.726 --> 35:06.966 align:middle
on the level of stock prices.

35:07.806 --> 35:12.306 align:middle
They may have been boosted by
expectations about tax policy;

35:12.666 --> 35:15.136 align:middle
possible cuts in
corporate tax rates

35:15.136 --> 35:20.716 align:middle
that have been much discussed;
or by expectations about growth,

35:20.796 --> 35:25.826 align:middle
possible reductions, and
downside risk to the economy.

35:26.166 --> 35:29.816 align:middle
But, you know, these are things

35:29.816 --> 35:32.476 align:middle
that market participants
are trying to view,

35:32.476 --> 35:37.086 align:middle
along with the likely path
of-paths of interest rates,

35:37.086 --> 35:38.806 align:middle
and I think all of that factors

35:38.806 --> 35:42.606 align:middle
into movements and
stock valuations.

35:42.866 --> 35:45.396 align:middle
But I don't-I don't
want to offer a view

35:45.396 --> 35:46.876 align:middle
as to whether they're
appropriate.

35:46.876 --> 35:47.556 align:middle
PETER BARNES.

35:48.406 --> 35:53.536 align:middle
You have said-mentioned-can
I just follow up?

35:53.646 --> 35:55.346 align:middle
On equity prices, you
have talked about whether

35:55.346 --> 35:57.356 align:middle
or not the valuations
are still-are

35:57.356 --> 36:00.506 align:middle
within historical
ranges and norms.

36:00.506 --> 36:04.606 align:middle
Did-is Dow 20,000 kind of
within historical norms?

36:04.606 --> 36:05.996 align:middle
Are you comfortable with that?

36:05.996 --> 36:06.456 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

36:06.456 --> 36:08.056 align:middle
Well, I think rates of return

36:08.656 --> 36:13.296 align:middle
in the stock market relative
to-remember that the level

36:13.296 --> 36:17.146 align:middle
of interest rates is low,
and, taking that into account,

36:17.576 --> 36:19.256 align:middle
I believe it's fair to say

36:19.256 --> 36:21.986 align:middle
that they remain
within normal ranges.

36:23.356 --> 36:29.746 align:middle
JOHN HELTMAN.

36:29.746 --> 36:31.736 align:middle
John Heltman of American Banker.

36:31.976 --> 36:35.256 align:middle
You said that much progress
has been made since the crisis

36:35.256 --> 36:39.256 align:middle
in making the banks more secure,
more safe, higher liquidity,

36:39.256 --> 36:40.486 align:middle
capital levels, et cetera.

36:40.896 --> 36:44.626 align:middle
Does that feeling extend to
the culture of the banks,

36:44.756 --> 36:47.816 align:middle
the way that they run,
the way that they operate

36:47.816 --> 36:51.446 align:middle
in their approach
towards-towards business?

36:51.446 --> 36:53.236 align:middle
Do you think that more
needs to be done there?

36:53.236 --> 36:56.526 align:middle
Do you expect that if
there has been improvement,

36:56.526 --> 37:00.716 align:middle
that it will continue to be-that
culture will continue to improve

37:01.256 --> 37:02.986 align:middle
in the next Administration
and the next Congress?

37:02.986 --> 37:03.666 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

37:04.156 --> 37:08.606 align:middle
Well, there have been many ways

37:08.606 --> 37:11.336 align:middle
in which there have
been compliance failures

37:11.336 --> 37:14.866 align:middle
at large organizations,
and you've seen a series

37:14.866 --> 37:21.586 align:middle
of enforcement actions by the
banking regulators over a range

37:21.586 --> 37:27.136 align:middle
of practices that suggests
some breakdown of compliance

37:27.136 --> 37:32.386 align:middle
or culture, whether it
involves violations of sanctions

37:32.386 --> 37:36.536 align:middle
or foreign exchange or
LIBOR or other matters.

37:36.536 --> 37:43.986 align:middle
So this is something that
we've discussed and emphasized,

37:43.986 --> 37:46.506 align:middle
the importance of a
culture of compliance.

37:46.846 --> 37:51.166 align:middle
And the Board will be
undertaking review.

37:51.536 --> 37:57.306 align:middle
We've already begun this
year, broadly, of compliance

37:57.306 --> 37:59.656 align:middle
and management of
compliance risk

37:59.836 --> 38:01.736 align:middle
in the largest corporations.

38:01.736 --> 38:05.516 align:middle
So I think this is
something that's important.

38:05.516 --> 38:08.806 align:middle
And the failings that
we have seen in a number

38:08.806 --> 38:12.896 align:middle
of institutions suggest there is
certainly room for improvement.

38:13.426 --> 38:16.056 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

38:17.856 --> 38:20.086 align:middle
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

38:20.476 --> 38:24.386 align:middle
I'd like to-two questions, if I
might-follow up on the question

38:24.386 --> 38:27.176 align:middle
about running the economy
hot that you mentioned

38:27.176 --> 38:29.636 align:middle
in your speech up in
Boston a couple months ago.

38:30.586 --> 38:31.366 align:middle
You brought it up.

38:31.366 --> 38:34.056 align:middle
Are you now sort of distancing
yourself from the idea?

38:34.056 --> 38:34.336 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

38:35.046 --> 38:38.126 align:middle
Well, I want to make clear what
I said and what I didn't say.

38:38.636 --> 38:44.246 align:middle
One of the things we've learned
from our financial crisis

38:44.246 --> 38:48.296 align:middle
and from a series of
financial crises in history

38:48.296 --> 38:51.966 align:middle
and around the globe
is that, very commonly,

38:52.246 --> 38:56.486 align:middle
when the economy takes a hit
and falls into a recession,

38:57.536 --> 39:01.326 align:middle
that productivity doesn't pick
up to pre-recession levels

39:01.666 --> 39:06.876 align:middle
and that there looks like there
is a permanent hit to the path

39:06.876 --> 39:09.526 align:middle
of potential output
for the economy,

39:09.936 --> 39:11.976 align:middle
which is called "hysteresis."

39:11.976 --> 39:14.346 align:middle
And I raised the
question as to whether

39:14.346 --> 39:19.136 align:middle
or not this might operate in
the opposite direction as well.

39:20.166 --> 39:23.826 align:middle
We have seen quite a few people

39:23.826 --> 39:28.046 align:middle
who lost their jobs during
the Great Recession drop

39:28.046 --> 39:33.636 align:middle
out of the labor force and,
you know, lose their ties

39:33.636 --> 39:36.206 align:middle
and become discouraged
and not reenter.

39:36.566 --> 39:39.556 align:middle
And I raised the
question as to whether

39:39.796 --> 39:44.016 align:middle
or not hysteresis could operate
in the opposite direction.

39:44.426 --> 39:48.086 align:middle
And that was a research
question for economists to see

39:48.516 --> 39:52.456 align:middle
if there is any evidence,
because it could be important

39:52.456 --> 39:53.936 align:middle
in determining policy.

39:54.236 --> 39:58.946 align:middle
But I didn't-I didn't draw any
policy conclusions from that.

39:59.246 --> 40:04.076 align:middle
And, as I indicated,
I think the path

40:04.076 --> 40:10.286 align:middle
that you see-that the Committee
has laid out in a tentative way

40:11.786 --> 40:17.186 align:middle
that involves a modest
degree of undershooting

40:17.186 --> 40:21.486 align:middle
of normal longer-run levels
of unemployment going down to

40:21.486 --> 40:25.886 align:middle
around 4½ percent would
remain for a couple

40:25.886 --> 40:28.486 align:middle
of years-might provide
some insight.

40:28.776 --> 40:34.966 align:middle
So I was not recommending a
substantially easier policy

40:34.966 --> 40:36.966 align:middle
in order to test
that hypothesis.

40:37.456 --> 40:39.166 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

40:39.356 --> 40:39.946 align:middle
Thank you, Madam Chair.

40:39.946 --> 40:43.376 align:middle
I guess my question to-that I
had before that follow-up was,

40:44.196 --> 40:47.056 align:middle
both times you've raised
rates in this cycle,

40:47.186 --> 40:48.726 align:middle
the market expectations

40:48.786 --> 40:50.756 align:middle
of a rate hike have
been over 50 percent.

40:51.236 --> 40:53.326 align:middle
The market has, you know,
kind of priced it in.

40:53.936 --> 40:55.816 align:middle
Is that a prerequisite
going forward?

40:56.116 --> 40:58.166 align:middle
Will that have to
happen as you go forward?

40:58.166 --> 40:58.686 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

40:58.686 --> 41:00.926 align:middle
It's not a prerequisite,
but we do try

41:00.926 --> 41:07.846 align:middle
to explain our evolving
views as clearly as we can.

41:08.356 --> 41:11.906 align:middle
It's important for market
participants to have a sense

41:11.906 --> 41:15.726 align:middle
of how we think about the
economy and the appropriate path

41:15.726 --> 41:18.956 align:middle
of policy, to look
at incoming data,

41:19.236 --> 41:24.776 align:middle
and to form their own judgments
as to whether or not changes

41:24.776 --> 41:26.786 align:middle
in policy would be appropriate.

41:27.066 --> 41:30.176 align:middle
And while it's not
a prerequisite,

41:30.596 --> 41:35.386 align:middle
if we're in good sync in
terms of explaining what goes

41:35.386 --> 41:39.536 align:middle
into our policy judgments,
it's not surprising

41:39.536 --> 41:40.906 align:middle
that it would often be true

41:40.906 --> 41:43.806 align:middle
that moves are well
anticipated by the market.

41:44.046 --> 41:48.486 align:middle
As I said, in thinking about
today's move, we've indicated

41:48.486 --> 41:51.686 align:middle
that we thought we were making
progress on our objectives.

41:51.876 --> 41:53.516 align:middle
We wanted to see some more.

41:53.806 --> 41:58.166 align:middle
I think anyone looking at
incoming data can clearly see

41:58.166 --> 42:01.356 align:middle
that we have enjoyed
further progress both

42:01.356 --> 42:03.606 align:middle
in the labor market
and on inflation.

42:03.926 --> 42:08.066 align:middle
And, therefore, there are good
reasons why the market should

42:08.066 --> 42:09.756 align:middle
have anticipated a moved today.

42:10.916 --> 42:12.796 align:middle
PATRICK GILLESPIE.

42:12.796 --> 42:14.586 align:middle
Chair Yellen, Patrick
Gillespie with CNN.

42:14.586 --> 42:16.006 align:middle
There's been a lot of discussion

42:16.006 --> 42:18.286 align:middle
about manufacturing
jobs and trade.

42:18.656 --> 42:21.646 align:middle
I'm wondering, what do you think
are two or three concrete steps

42:21.936 --> 42:24.886 align:middle
that could be taken to increase
manufacturing employment

42:24.886 --> 42:25.676 align:middle
in the United States?

42:25.676 --> 42:27.666 align:middle
And, separately, I've asked
you about the importance

42:27.666 --> 42:28.766 align:middle
of job training programs,

42:28.766 --> 42:30.116 align:middle
something that you've
emphasized.

42:30.116 --> 42:32.766 align:middle
Specifically, what do you think

42:32.766 --> 42:35.496 align:middle
about the worker assistance
provided to workers

42:35.496 --> 42:38.376 align:middle
who lose their jobs to trade,
both the financial assistance

42:38.376 --> 42:40.556 align:middle
and the worker retraining
programs?

42:40.556 --> 42:41.486 align:middle
Are they sufficient?

42:41.766 --> 42:42.106 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

42:43.326 --> 42:44.876 align:middle
So, I don't want to weigh in.

42:44.876 --> 42:46.206 align:middle
I don't intend to weigh in.

42:46.206 --> 42:49.046 align:middle
I haven't weighed in on
either fiscal policy,

42:49.046 --> 42:52.536 align:middle
specifics of evaluating
policies.

42:52.786 --> 42:56.636 align:middle
I'm not going to weigh
in either on the details

42:56.636 --> 42:59.506 align:middle
of particular trade policies.

42:59.926 --> 43:02.686 align:middle
But, more generally, I'd say,

43:02.686 --> 43:05.596 align:middle
you asked me about
manufacturing.

43:05.986 --> 43:11.176 align:middle
There were a lot of
manufacturing jobs lost

43:11.176 --> 43:12.896 align:middle
over a long period of time

43:12.896 --> 43:16.876 align:middle
and particularly after-during
the Great Recession.

43:17.306 --> 43:22.276 align:middle
We've had some recovery in
manufacturing employment

43:22.276 --> 43:24.146 align:middle
as the economy's recovered.

43:24.556 --> 43:28.886 align:middle
So, to some extent,
manufacturing employment depends

43:28.886 --> 43:34.216 align:middle
on the progress of the economy,
and we have seen some recovery.

43:34.776 --> 43:37.816 align:middle
Over a long period of time,

43:38.116 --> 43:40.566 align:middle
technological change
is something

43:40.566 --> 43:42.836 align:middle
that has been important

43:42.836 --> 43:47.886 align:middle
in reducing manufacturing
employment absolutely

43:47.886 --> 43:50.976 align:middle
and as a share of
jobs in the economy.

43:51.246 --> 43:55.076 align:middle
For decades, the pace
of technological change

43:55.076 --> 44:00.066 align:middle
in manufacturing has outstripped
that in the economy as a whole.

44:00.506 --> 44:07.976 align:middle
And, so, firms-manufacturing
firms have found it easier

44:07.976 --> 44:13.726 align:middle
to continue producing by-with
reducing their workforces.

44:14.016 --> 44:17.786 align:middle
And that's a change that I
would expect to continue.

44:19.456 --> 44:21.706 align:middle
You mentioned worker assistance.

44:21.936 --> 44:25.076 align:middle
So some of the forces that
are acting on manufacturing

44:25.076 --> 44:28.406 align:middle
and other sectors,
including technological change

44:28.406 --> 44:34.046 align:middle
and globalization-even though
most economists would judge the

44:34.046 --> 44:38.236 align:middle
overall consequences of these
developments for the economy

44:38.536 --> 44:42.926 align:middle
to be positive, certainly
there are groups of workers

44:42.926 --> 44:46.236 align:middle
who were harmed by
developments pertaining

44:46.236 --> 44:48.676 align:middle
to globalization and technology.

44:49.096 --> 44:53.956 align:middle
And I think most economists
and policymakers recognize

44:54.266 --> 45:00.036 align:middle
that it's important to provide
ways for workers who were harmed

45:00.036 --> 45:05.406 align:middle
by these kinds of developments
to be retrained for jobs

45:05.406 --> 45:08.536 align:middle
so that they can
succeed in the economy.

45:08.846 --> 45:13.286 align:middle
And so, I mean, I would
agree-without getting

45:13.286 --> 45:17.176 align:middle
into any particular program
and whether it's sufficient,

45:17.606 --> 45:21.926 align:middle
I do think that it's important
to recognize there are those

45:22.136 --> 45:24.216 align:middle
who were harmed by
these developments,

45:24.486 --> 45:28.816 align:middle
and their concerns, I
think, need to be addressed

45:28.846 --> 45:30.456 align:middle
by policymakers, certainly.

45:31.806 --> 45:34.586 align:middle
JUSTINE UNDERHILL.

45:34.726 --> 45:36.356 align:middle
Justine Underhill,
Yahoo Finance.

45:36.856 --> 45:40.066 align:middle
So the Fed's balance sheet
has grown to over $4 trillion.

45:40.176 --> 45:43.106 align:middle
And as the Fed begins
removing policy accommodation,

45:43.416 --> 45:45.996 align:middle
under what circumstances
would you see the Fed removing

45:46.326 --> 45:49.786 align:middle
or possibly winding
down its balance sheet

45:50.356 --> 45:53.016 align:middle
in either letting
mature-securities mature

45:53.016 --> 45:56.756 align:middle
or possibly outright selling
bonds from the SOMA portfolio?

45:56.976 --> 45:57.216 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

45:57.746 --> 46:01.396 align:middle
So we've indicated in our
normalization principles

46:01.696 --> 46:06.206 align:middle
that we expect to diminish
the size of our portfolio

46:06.206 --> 46:11.256 align:middle
over time largely by
ceasing reinvestments

46:11.256 --> 46:15.226 align:middle
of principal rather than
by selling securities.

46:16.476 --> 46:20.026 align:middle
We've indicated that once
the process of normalization

46:20.026 --> 46:22.596 align:middle
of the federal funds
rate is well under way,

46:23.006 --> 46:28.156 align:middle
we would probably begin to
allow our portfolio to run off.

46:28.266 --> 46:31.476 align:middle
We've not yet made
any precise decisions

46:31.726 --> 46:33.546 align:middle
about when that will occur.

46:33.546 --> 46:39.286 align:middle
We want to feel that
if the economy were

46:39.286 --> 46:43.426 align:middle
to suffer an adverse shock,
that we have some scope

46:43.796 --> 46:47.286 align:middle
through traditional means
of interest rate cuts

46:47.376 --> 46:49.326 align:middle
to be able to respond to that.

46:49.326 --> 46:53.266 align:middle
Now, there's no mechanical
rule about what level

46:53.266 --> 46:56.996 align:middle
of the federal funds rate
we might deem appropriate

46:56.996 --> 46:58.926 align:middle
to begin that process.

46:58.926 --> 47:01.896 align:middle
It's not something that
only depends on the level

47:01.896 --> 47:03.286 align:middle
of the federal funds rate.

47:03.286 --> 47:07.846 align:middle
It also depends on our judgment
of the amount of momentum

47:07.846 --> 47:11.546 align:middle
in the economy and
possible concerns

47:11.546 --> 47:14.836 align:middle
about downside risks
to the economy.

47:15.246 --> 47:19.596 align:middle
So we've not yet made this
decision, but it is something

47:19.596 --> 47:21.356 align:middle
that we have long planned,

47:21.646 --> 47:25.266 align:middle
to begin to allow our
balance sheet to run off.

47:25.266 --> 47:27.746 align:middle
And then it would
take several years.

47:28.236 --> 47:30.926 align:middle
And we would end up,
if all goes well,

47:30.926 --> 47:33.736 align:middle
with a substantially
smaller balance sheet

47:33.806 --> 47:34.946 align:middle
than we have at present.

47:35.986 --> 47:36.616 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

47:37.436 --> 47:41.976 align:middle
Mike Derby from Dow
Jones Newswires.

47:42.536 --> 47:45.606 align:middle
I wondered if the unexpected
outcome of the election

47:45.646 --> 47:48.366 align:middle
and the sense that a lot
of people are really upset

47:48.366 --> 47:52.726 align:middle
with how the economy is
performing despite having,

47:52.726 --> 47:54.966 align:middle
you know, aggregate
economic statistics

47:54.966 --> 47:58.356 align:middle
that look pretty good-is
that causing you in any way

47:58.356 --> 48:02.396 align:middle
to think differently about
how you evaluate the economy,

48:02.396 --> 48:04.426 align:middle
like what sort of things
you look for to get a sense

48:04.426 --> 48:06.176 align:middle
of what's going on
in the economy?

48:07.096 --> 48:10.976 align:middle
Is-you know, basically,
did how things turned

48:10.976 --> 48:12.836 align:middle
out in the election-is it
making you think differently

48:12.836 --> 48:15.186 align:middle
about how you evaluate
the economy's performance

48:15.186 --> 48:15.926 align:middle
and how it's doing?

48:16.466 --> 48:17.756 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

48:17.896 --> 48:22.716 align:middle
Well, I mean, we've long
been aware, and I've spoken

48:22.716 --> 48:26.756 align:middle
about previously disturbing
trends in the economy,

48:26.836 --> 48:31.996 align:middle
particularly rising wage
inequality, income inequality,

48:32.356 --> 48:34.776 align:middle
and the fact that
a significant share

48:35.066 --> 48:40.016 align:middle
of our population hasn't been
enjoying significant real wage

48:40.076 --> 48:41.216 align:middle
gains, if any.

48:41.696 --> 48:45.136 align:middle
And so these are
long-standing concerns.

48:45.846 --> 48:49.186 align:middle
These are not new phenomenon,

48:49.296 --> 48:53.106 align:middle
but the recession
was very severe

48:53.376 --> 48:56.836 align:middle
and probably exacerbated
developments

48:56.836 --> 49:01.756 align:middle
that had long been affecting
many American workers

49:01.756 --> 49:02.696 align:middle
and households.

49:03.036 --> 49:05.356 align:middle
And I think they are
quite disturbing.

49:05.666 --> 49:11.896 align:middle
Now, they're ones that the Fed
is not well positioned-I think

49:11.896 --> 49:14.786 align:middle
our policies can
affect the general level

49:15.226 --> 49:20.076 align:middle
of economic activity and
slack in the labor market,

49:20.286 --> 49:24.396 align:middle
the level-the rate of
inflation, which we focus on.

49:24.626 --> 49:28.256 align:middle
But I think it's important
for policymakers more broadly

49:28.316 --> 49:33.426 align:middle
to be attentive to these trends
and to think about policies

49:33.746 --> 49:35.696 align:middle
that could address them.

49:36.066 --> 49:38.976 align:middle
We've been quite
attentive with respect

49:39.076 --> 49:43.446 align:middle
to particular demographic
groups in the labor market.

49:43.786 --> 49:46.746 align:middle
Particularly, minorities tend

49:46.746 --> 49:50.276 align:middle
to be very badly
affected by downturns.

49:50.596 --> 49:52.616 align:middle
We've discussed that.

49:52.666 --> 49:54.806 align:middle
We've been focused
on it-it's not just

49:54.806 --> 49:57.726 align:middle
since the election-and
are pleased to see

49:58.056 --> 50:00.106 align:middle
that they are enjoying gains.

50:00.106 --> 50:03.436 align:middle
For example, the African
American unemployment rate

50:03.436 --> 50:09.316 align:middle
at this point is now rough-about
back to 2007 levels as well.

50:09.686 --> 50:15.296 align:middle
But these are important trends,
and I think it's important

50:15.296 --> 50:18.146 align:middle
for policy to address them.

